    "body": "corsAttributeMode should probably be corsAttributeState to match the definition\n\nThe \"CORS settings attributes\" \"Brief Description\" column no longer reads correctly. It should say something like the current text, e.g. \"Requests for the element will have their mode set to `\"cors\"` and credentials mode...\"\n\nMight want to replace \"XHR\" with \"Fetch\" in the \"Where does this specification fit?\" diagram in the introduction.\n\nAfter \"new resource is to be fetched using\"... the change from \"HTTP GET\" to ``GET`` doesn't seem helpful, as you're not specifically talking about a byte sequence. Similar elsewhere. You're talking about the GET HTTP method.\n\nFor \"This still needs to be integrated with the Fetch standard\" be sure to open an issue or keep #95 open. \n\n``` diff\n-  <p>When a user agent is to handle a resource obtained from a <span>fetch</span> algorithm <dfn>as\n-  a download</dfn>, it should provide the user with a way to save the resource for later use, if a\n-  resource is successfully obtained; or otherwise should report any problems downloading the file to\n-  the user.</p>\n+  <p>When a user agent is to handle a resource obtained from a fetch <dfn>as a download</dfn>, it\n+  should provide the user with a way to save the resource for later use, if a resource is\n+  successfully obtained; or otherwise should report any problems downloading the file to the\n+  user.</p>\n```\n\nDid you mean to omit the linkification of fetch here?\n\n\"And request must include a Ping-From header\" omit \"And\"\n\nUsage of backticks for header names seems inconsistent, unless there is logic I am missing.\n\n> This may be done in parallel with the primary fetch, and is independent of the result of that fetch.\n\nDoes \"the primary fetch\" have a definition you can link to?\n\n---\n\nThat's as far as I got to today...\n",
