    "body": "@domenic I called it corsAttributeMode because that matches how the standard often already talks about this. E.g., \"CORS mode\".\n\n> The \"CORS settings attributes\" \"Brief Description\" column no longer reads correctly.\n\nThis was updated. Could you be more specific?\n\n> You're talking about the GET HTTP method.\n\nI'm just making the language consistent with Fetch. 1) Fetch generalizes requests and therefore methods too beyond HTTP. E.g., they have an effect on data URLs (mostly network error). 2) Fetch always marks them up as a byte sequence since that is what they are. 3) Note that HTML originally used \"or equivalent\" language here which we can remove because Fetch now has that abstraction.\n\n> Did you mean to omit the linkification of fetch here?\n\nYes, I removed explicit references to fetch a bunch of times since it wasn't done consistently to begin with. There's a bunch of follow up work to be done to further clean that up and use more of the response terminology from Fetch. I was planning on filing a post-Fetch integration issue on that so we could work through that. This initial PR takes care of clarifying the request side mostly.\n\n> Usage of backticks for header names seems inconsistent, unless there is logic I am missing.\n\nI would like to make this more consistent in a follow up. I could do it as part of this PR if you think that helps, but thought it would be too much.\n\n> Does \"the primary fetch\" have a definition you can link to?\n\nThe original \"primary request\" didn't either. Seems like something we need to improve separately.\n",
