    "created_at": "2015-09-16T15:21:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-16T15:21:04Z",
    "body": "> I called it corsAttributeMode because that matches how the standard often already talks about this. E.g., \"CORS mode\".\n\nThen maybe the section https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#cors-settings-attributes should be updated to stop using \"state\" and use \"mode\" instead.\n\n> This was updated. Could you be more specific?\n\nI thought I was. The sentence structure should be more like the current text. The use of \"whose\" is strange. 'Requests for the element whose mode is \"cors\" and credentials mode is \"same-origin'\" is not a complete sentence. It's just a subject of a sentence.\n\n> I'm just making the language consistent with Fetch.\n\nI still think there's a distinction between e.g. \"HTTP GET requests\" and \"a request whose method is ``GET``\". But no big deal.\n\n> I would like to make this more consistent in a follow up. I could do it as part of this PR if you think that helps, but thought it would be too much.\n\nI think it would be better to not use backticks at all for headers for now and do one follow-up that uniformizes it everywhere.\n",
